{
  "task": "task-a9397847",
  "findings": [
    "The marked area consists of two separate 4-row noisy patterns: rows 6–9 and 21–24 (0-based). Their right sides share a structure: rows 6–9, columns 15–21 form solid/alternating/solid/alternating 4s; rows 21–24, columns 18–20 form the corresponding 3-column pattern.",
    "The upper pattern reaches the right edge, so it has no right-edge suffix to extend. In the lower pattern, column 21 is background in all four rows. The visually strongest rectangle-extension candidates are at row 21 and row 23, where the preceding 4s form solid horizontal runs; rows 22 and 24 are alternating, interrupted rows and are weaker candidates.",
    "The rejected candidate that changed column 21 in all four lower rows likely overincluded rows 22 and 24. This is a geometry-based hypothesis, not confirmed by the benchmark's binary feedback."
  ],
  "likely_previous_errors": {
    "overincluded": "Potentially rows 22 and 24, column 21 (0-based), in the full-height lower suffix fill.",
    "omitted": "If the qualifying solid-run interpretation is right, rows 21 and 23, column 21 are the likely cells to add.",
    "preservation": "Avoid clearing or recoloring the existing 4-pattern as generic noise; the demos preserve patterned cells except where the validated transformation specifically changes them."
  },
  "coordinates": "All row and column references above are 0-based.",
  "files_created_or_modified": [],
  "issues": "The prior submissions were rejected, but feedback identified no specific wrong cells; the proposed distinction between solid and alternating rows remains uncertain."
}

{
  "what_i_did": "Reviewed the six demo pairs, the test grid, and the earlier rejected submissions in the prior transcript.",
  "what_i_found": {
    "shared_effect": "Every demo changes a selected cell or right-side patch to color 1; examples range from one cell (demo 1) to a 4x5 patch (demo 2).",
    "why_simple_guesses_failed": "The changed area is not consistently the last column, every row suffix, or always the top or bottom noisy block. The test has two separated, similar 4x22 patterned blocks, so extending based only on scattered 4s or apparent row ends can select noise or the wrong region. The rejected guesses reflect that ambiguity.",
    "likely_rule": "The evidence points toward identifying a genuine 2D patterned component amid noisy cells, then marking its rightward continuation in color 1. The exact criterion for distinguishing that component from noise is not established confidently by the six pairs."
  },
  "recommendation": {
    "request_another_demo": true,
    "reason": "Four submissions have failed and two demo requests remain. A further example that clearly contrasts a genuine 2D component with isolated noise could resolve the selection rule before another guess."
  },
  "files_created_or_modified": [],
  "issues": "The demos support a selected rightward fill, but do not uniquely determine how to choose the target component in this test."
}
